body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `writeJson` utility function does not check if the `value` parameter is `undefined`. If `undefined` is passed, `JSON.stringify(undefined)` returns `undefined`, resulting in writing `"undefined\n"` to the output file, which is invalid JSON. Adding a defensive check for `value === undefined` will prevent this.

```suggestion
function writeJson(root, relPath, value) {
  const target = resolvePath(root, relPath);
  if (!target) throw new TypeError('root and relPath must be strings');
  if (value === undefined) throw new TypeError('value must not be undefined');
  fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(target), { recursive: true });
  fs.writeFileSync(target, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\n`);
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When writing artifacts, ensure that missing or undefined inputs fail with a controlled error (e.g., 'artifacts must be an object') before any write operations, and use optional chaining for safe default evaluation.
</details>

