body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using `JSON.stringify` for array comparisons is strictly order-sensitive and fragile. If the order of `required_error_class_ids` in the packet changes, the validation will fail even if all required IDs are present. Using an order-insensitive check is much more robust.

```javascript
  const hasAllRequired = isObject(packet.error_taxonomy_context)
    && Array.isArray(packet.error_taxonomy_context.required_error_class_ids)
    && packet.error_taxonomy_context.required_error_class_ids.length === PILOT_ERROR_TAXONOMY_CLASS_IDS.length
    && PILOT_ERROR_TAXONOMY_CLASS_IDS.every((id) => packet.error_taxonomy_context.required_error_class_ids.includes(id));
  if (!hasAllRequired) {
    failures.push('packet_error_taxonomy_context_required');
  }
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Avoid using `JSON.stringify` for array comparisons in tests because it is strictly order-sensitive. Instead, use order-insensitive assertions, such as verifying the array length and checking element presence with `includes` or set-based checks.
</details>
